---
import DocsPage from "../../layouts/DocsPage.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DocsPage id="mcp-and-plugins">

          <p>
            MCP servers and project connections give an agent structured access to approved tools. Vector's catalog
            has 135 connectors — GitHub, Linear, Sentry, Figma, Playwright, Postgres, Stripe, Slack and more — plus any
            MCP server you add yourself.
          </p>

          <h2 id="connections">Connect a server</h2>
          <p>
            Open <strong>Plugins</strong> or <strong>Connections</strong> from the workspace rail. Add only services you
            trust, confirm the permissions they request, and verify the connection before depending on it in a task.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            A listed integration is not automatically connected. It becomes available only after its
            setup succeeds and Vector reports it as active.
          </p>
          <ul class="feature-list">
            <li><strong>Plugin runtimes that install themselves</strong><span>A catalog plugin that needs uvx no longer becomes a terminal errand. Vector resolves the runner, installs it through Astral's own installer when it is missing, and rewrites the command to an absolute path so it spawns without a login shell.</span></li>
            <li><strong>Servers that stop answering</strong><span>MCP servers that stop answering are recovered instead of wedging the app.</span></li>
          </ul>

          <h2 id="cli-mcp">Custom MCP servers from the terminal</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector mcp add</code> registers a server: <code>--url</code> for a remote one with <code>--header KEY=VALUE</code>, or a command with <code>--env KEY=VALUE</code> for a local one. <code>vector mcp list</code> shows every configured server and whether it is reachable.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector mcp add linear --url https://mcp.linear.app/sse
vector mcp auth linear
vector mcp list</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            For OAuth-protected servers, <code>vector mcp auth</code> runs the flow, <code>vector mcp auth list</code> shows which servers are OAuth-capable and their status, <code>vector mcp logout</code> drops stored credentials, and <code>vector mcp debug &lt;name&gt;</code> diagnoses a failing connection.
          </p>

          <h2 id="cli-plugins">Plugins</h2>
          <p>
            <code>vector plugin &lt;module&gt;</code> installs an npm module as a plugin and writes it into your config; <code>--global</code> installs into the global config rather than the project's, and <code>--force</code> replaces a version already there. A plugin can register extra tools, which the registry merges alongside the built-in ones.
          </p>
          <p class="doc-cmd"><code>vector plugin @acme/vector-plugin -g</code><button class="doc-cmd-copy" type="button" aria-label="Copy this command">Copy</button></p>
          <p>
            For new settings, use Vector names: <code>vector.json</code>,
            <code>vector.jsonc</code>, <code>.vector/</code>, and <code>VECTOR_*</code> variables, which 1.99.91, the next release, is the first to read. It recognizes earlier config files and project folders by their contents,
            imports them into Vector paths once, and preserves originals. Ambiguous or conflicting security
            settings require repair before startup. Plugins import <code>@vectordevai/plugin</code>, including its
            <code>/tui</code> export. Local plugins and tools can keep a scoped SDK import when that module is unavailable;
            Vector supplies its own SDK without replacing a dependency that already resolves. New and published
            plugins should use the Vector imports explicitly. Theme manifests prefer <code>vector-themes</code>;
            keys ending in <code>-themes</code> remain accepted with the same path validation. The package will be published
            before the CLI release that installs it.
          </p>
          <p class="note">
            <code>--pure</code> on any command runs without external plugins. It is the first thing to try when you suspect one of breaking a run.
          </p>
</DocsPage>
